#################################################################
#  ADOPTION PROFILE — owner-decided, reasoned, dated, directional
#################################################################




#################################################################
#  DEFINITION OF DONE — the bar this development is held to
#################################################################


#################################################################
#  DECOMPOSITION — what the remaining epics actually consist of.
#  Absent from v1.0.0: four scored epics broken into nothing, which
#  is why a mid-build decision had nowhere to be settled in advance.
#  Each story is scoreable in its own right; per the framework's own
#  rule the parent epics carry scores and these do not, so nothing
#  double-counts. They are notYetScoreable with the reason recorded.
#################################################################




#################################################################
#  v5.1.0 — STAGE 2: THE SCOPE. Deliverables and exclusions only.
#
#  The Mission stage closed at commit f2f4e0f and its digest is
#  backfilled above from the real commit. This commit closes the
#  Scope stage: what the boundary REQUIRES, read clause by clause
#  from the owner's mission, with no goals, no objectives and no
#  backlog. Those close in later commits.
#
#  Five deliverables, each quoting the mission clause it came from
#  — the check added at v1.90.0 after the previous scope was found
#  wider than its mission.
#################################################################







#################################################################
#  v5.2.0 — STAGE 2 REVISITED. The scope now says WHERE.
#
#  OWNER FINDING: the deliverables written at v1.100.0 were the
#  mission restated — "governance is expressed as ontology" is the
#  mission's own sentence with the subject changed. A scope must
#  say what should be DONE and WHERE, and that one did neither.
#
#  CAUSE: the framework, not only the session. ScopeDeliverable is
#  defined as "a deliverable says WHAT MUST BE TRUE". Applied to a
#  mission already stated as conditions, that definition can only
#  produce restatement. ScopeArea is added to carry the WHERE.
#
#  EXCLUSIONS WITHDRAWN. Ex_NoNarrativeBan, Ex_NoEngineRewrite and
#  Ex_NoReasonerMandate were written before anyone had established
#  what the mission needs — narrowing a boundary before knowing
#  what it must cover. They may all three be right; they were
#  decided in the wrong order, and an exclusion once written reads
#  as settled. They return at the Ruling stage if the coverage
#  analysis still supports them.
#################################################################

#  Del_CodeExecutes has no area of its own: it is the finish
#  condition for Area_RuleExecution and Area_CodeTables together,
#  and inventing a fifth area to give it one would be padding the
#  scope to make the shape look tidy.


#  The L2 clause is right: a boundary with no exclusion refuses
#  nothing. What was wrong at v1.100.0 was WHICH exclusions and in
#  what order — three written before any area was known. One is
#  restored now, because the coverage analysis above establishes it
#  directly rather than assuming it: the four areas name documents
#  and modules, and none of them is "all prose everywhere". The
#  other two return at the Ruling stage or not at all.

#  Scope_Build predates ScopeArea and has none. Its areas are
#  recorded from what its deliverables actually touched, so the new
#  clause is satisfied by history rather than by exempting it.

#  The previous lineage's goals, given facings retroactively. Both
#  are mission-facing: they state conditions about the framework,
#  not about areas — which is itself the finding, since that lineage
#  had no scope-facing or containment-facing goal at all and could
#  not have reported either failure.






#  The two withdrawn meta-goals, recorded as what they actually are.

#  The product-layer area the audit found missing: what the
#  framework will DO differently once the work is done.


#  ProductScopeKind test-drive (proposed by code-abundance-rdodi, adopted
#  v1.63.0): the framework's own two Layer_Product deliverables, classified
#  on their existing text, not reworded to fit. Del_OntGovernance claims a
#  capability the ontology has (machine-checkable rulings exist); Del_OntRuleExec
#  claims a reliability guarantee about how rule execution behaves once it does
#  — a quality, not a new capability. A real mixed result, matching the
#  proposal's own verification method.

#  Exclusions in BOTH layers, so the scope can refuse a capability
#  as well as a piece of work.

#  Inverse goals: one per exclusion, measurable by absence.




#################################################################
#  The previous lineage's objectives, given a measurement kind and
#  a closing checkpoint retroactively.
#
#  Recorded as RETROSPECTIVE: a checkpoint set after the work is
#  finished cannot predict anything, and the honest thing is to
#  place it at the deadline with the value that was actually
#  reached. It makes the objective conformant and it does not
#  pretend the objective was monitored. That distinction is the
#  whole point of adding checkpoints, so blurring it here would
#  undo the release.
#################################################################







#  The two inverse objectives are movable by every epic, because
#  each one is a chance to breach them.











#################################################################
#  v5.9.0 — packages, sprint plans and kick-off, before any work.
#
#  Measured first: 9 stories, 27 points, and the last closed
#  iteration declared a capacity of 9. Twenty-seven does not fit
#  one box, so the work is split across three — G9, and the same
#  arithmetic that opened It6 last time.
#
#  Stories are taken in SCORE ORDER: EP21 at 8.00 before EP22 and
#  EP23 at 6.00, before EP24 at 4.00 and EP25 at 3.00. The order
#  falls out of the register rather than being chosen.
#
#  Two packages, grouped by capability rather than by time box:
#  what an adopter can RECEIVE separately.
#################################################################







#  The nine stories made ready: specification, ordered steps, a
#  state change, a test case and its data. Nothing here is new
#  vocabulary — all of it shipped at v1.97.0 and went unused.










#  The state space, declared by analysis rather than invented per
#  story — the v1.98.0 rule that a state name must come from an
#  artefact some analysis task produced.


#################################################################
#  v6.1.0 — the owner's grooming model applied.
#
#  Tested against what was built and found two deviations plus a
#  framework gap:
#
#   1. Epics carried acceptance criteria and NO Definition of Done
#      and NO test cases. The stories were therefore written to
#      satisfy nothing stated at epic level.
#   2. All nine stories were detailed in one pass at v1.109.0,
#      three sprints ahead — not per sprint.
#   3. The framework could not express (2): a RefinementEvent
#      recorded WHEN grooming happened, never WHICH iteration it
#      prepared for. Just-in-time grooming could be performed and
#      never checked.
#
#  Each epic now carries a DoD and a test case exercising its
#  criterion — the target the stories are converted to satisfy.
#################################################################

#  Grooming attributed to the iteration it prepared for. The nine
#  refinements written at v1.109.0 are attributed HONESTLY: all
#  were performed on 27 October, and It9 starts 30 November, so
#  three of them will raise the ahead-of-time advisory. That is the
#  correct report — the detail for It9 was written five weeks early
#  and the register should say so rather than hide it.

#################################################################
#  The previous lineage's epics, given the DoD and test cases the
#  owner's model requires.
#
#  Not exempted: Mission_BuildSoftware_v2 is LIVE. The lineage is
#  finished and its mission still stands, so the rule applies and
#  the epics were genuinely missing what it asks for.
#
#  Recorded as RETROACTIVE. Each DoD states what was in fact
#  required of that epic, and each test case names the fixture that
#  already attests it — the evidence existed, and what was missing
#  was the epic-level statement of what the stories were converted
#  to satisfy.
#################################################################
fw:Ruling_G1 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G1" ;
    rdfs:label "Granularity by momentum"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "The first class reached for becomes the default and is never revisited. One session ran fifteen turns with 29 work items, every one an Epic, and found it only when directly asked to audit. *Surfaced by* LineageDepthAdvisoryShape — which is not level-gated and fires at L1. If it never fired for you, the full shapes file was never run." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:LineageDepthAdvisoryShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G2 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G2" ;
    rdfs:label "Advisory blindness"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "A warning nobody reads protects nothing. The validator groups advisories by message rather than printing a total, because *'175 Warning'* is a number a reader learns to skip. L4 exists because this boundary is not solvable by better wording." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:AdvisoryVisibilityShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G3 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G3" ;
    rdfs:label "Permitted is not intended"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Epic ⊑ ProductBacklogItem is true, and does not license planning an undecomposed epic into an iteration. A subclass relation answers what may be asserted; a definition answers what the term means. Two sessions made this error from opposite ends within a day. *Enforced by* EpicPlanningShape at L2." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:PermittedNotIntendedShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G4 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G4" ;
    rdfs:label "Completion is not accomplishment"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Completion is a fact about effort; accomplishment is a fact about the world, and only the second requires a measurement. A register of Done items proves the first and says nothing about the second. *Enforced by* L4MeasuredObjectiveShape: every objective carries a MetricObservation, whatever the reading said." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:CommitmentShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G5 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G5" ;
    rdfs:label "Why, when, and what are three questions"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Epic answers why, Iteration answers when, DeploymentUnit answers what users received. Conflating them is the commonest drift observed here: an epic in a sprint looks like planning and commits nothing anyone can finish; an epic in a release names something that cannot ship. *Enforced by* L4GroomingShape." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:IterationMembershipShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G6 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G6" ;
    rdfs:label "Drift is the default, not the exception"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Corollary from v3.0.0: drift is *detected* identically whichever order the chain was built in — L4DriftShape fires on an item pursuing an unrealised objective either way, verified by construction on both. What the order changes is when a human notices: scope-first surfaces the conflict while the objective is being written, scope-last surfaces it only once work exists to be rejected. Work" ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:EvidenceShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G7 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G7" ;
    rdfs:label "A tool that refuses is not thereby correct"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Three defects in this framework's own tooling were plausible refusals or meaningless clean passes: a cumulative flow reading a property that never existed, a burn-down reaching zero over open work, a date comparison reporting future deadlines as passed. Each *looked* like the tool working. *No shape catches this.* The check that does is a fixture whose answer is known in advance." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:ScoreFreshnessShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G18 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G18" ;
    rdfs:label "The lineage is a pipeline; order is enforced by artifact, not by claim"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "An earlier release concluded that execution order could not be gated. That was wrong on two counts, and the owner named both. The v1.62.0 exclusion forbids requiring a fixed-at *date* and says nothing about enforcing order; and an ontology has dependency relations, through which order can be enforced. The model is another registrant's: each stage consumes the artifact the previous stage produced. A St" ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:StageOutputShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G17 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G17" ;
    rdfs:label "A scope must have content of its own, or the backlog defines it"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Writing the scope before the goals is not enough. A scope of prose has nothing to measure work against, so coverage is computed over the work that happens to exist — and both sides of the fraction become the backlog. Measured here: Obj_ScopeDelivered counted stories pursuing an objective under the scope and divided by the same set. It read 100% whether the scope was satisfied or merely emp" ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:ScopeContentShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G13 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G13" ;
    rdfs:label "The chain is Mission → Scope → Goals → Objectives, and it reads the same both ways"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Goals are derived from the scope, not attached to the mission. The scope is built to satisfy the mission; goals are derived from the scope so that *the scope's fit to the mission is what gets tested*; objectives are set to measure the goals. Read downward or upward, the same answer must come out. The failure this prevents was structural and invisible for the whole life of this package: Goal " ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:GoalDerivationShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G14 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G14" ;
    rdfs:label "Every intent element records who authored it, not only the mission"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "MissionOrigin was added at v1.70.0 after a session wrote five missions and attributed them to the owner. It was applied to Mission and nowhere else — so the same failure moved one level down and recurred *in the release that corrected it*: the same session then wrote the scope, the goals and the objectives beneath the corrected mission, and attributed those to the owner too. A fix applie" ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:IntentOriginShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G15 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G15" ;
    rdfs:label "Pursuing an objective and being able to move it are different claims"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "pursuesObjective records intent. metricMovableBy records capability. A register can reach every epic Done with every objective unmet and flag nothing, because no constraint ever asked whether the work beneath an objective could shift its metric. When they disagree, adjust the backlog, not the objective. Re-targeting an objective to meet the work is moving the goalpost to meet the shot." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:MetricReachabilityShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G16 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G16" ;
    rdfs:label "Development is anchored on packages, not iterations"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Work reaches users through regularly deployable packages built from the highest-priority items. A register where completion and delivery are separate records measures the first and assumes the second. Measured here: 43 of 47 Done items sat in no package, while the framework shipped 78 real releases its own register never recorded." ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:DeploymentCoverageShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G11 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G11" ;
    rdfs:label "Run at the level you enforce, or the rules are decoration"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "A package that declares a conformance level below the one its own framework enforces exempts itself from the rules it publishes. Measured on this package: 16 of 57 level-gated constraints did not run and 166 violations were invisible, including fourteen forked chains that the L4 rule *written to catch them* could not see. This is the mechanism behind every drift in this package's history. " ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:SelfExemptionShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G12 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G12" ;
    rdfs:label "A superseded lineage is a record, not a claim"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "When a mission is superseded, the chain beneath it stays. Re-pointing goals to remove an inconsistency would assert an intent never held — the fabrication L-112 forbids one level up. Constraints on chain integrity must therefore exempt superseded lineage: the defect is real, stays visible, and must not block a live release. The corollary is the warning: fourteen forks arose because missions were" ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:ChainClosureShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G9 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G9" ;
    rdfs:label "A constant iteration, and stories split to fit it"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "An iteration is a fixed time box. Sizing it to the longest story inverts the control: if its length is set by the work it contains, you always fit, and velocity becomes a tautology that can never tell you that you did not. Assessing iteration length after story-writing fails for the same reason one step later — the next change produces a bigger story and the question reopens. Set the iterati" ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:StoryIterationFitShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G10 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G10" ;
    rdfs:label "Publish each increment, do not batch"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "A release gate that passes and is not then published leaves the governed store behind the work, and two unpublished increments cannot be separated afterwards into the releases they should have been. Publish immediately after each gate PASS, before starting the next increment. Its corollary is easy to miss: a release gate that cannot finish blocks every release. This package's gate grew to " ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:DistributionDriftShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .
fw:Ruling_G8 a backlog:GovernanceRuling ;
    backlog:hasRulingIdentifier "G8" ;
    rdfs:label "Every rule naming a member of a closed set depends on that set's membership"@en ;
    backlog:hasRulingStatement "Adding L4_LineageEnforced to a four-member enumeration broke three rules that had tested *'below L3'* by name — they fired on L4, which is above it. *No shape catches this either.* It was caught because a fixture existed that exercised the new member, which is G7 applied to vocabulary. --- - Read the standard in full before the first work item, SHA-verified — not a fragment, not a prior sess" ;
    backlog:enforcedByShape backlog:DecompositionShape ;
    backlog:rulingSource "LINEAGE_OPERATING_DISCIPLINE, ruled by the owner in session." .

#  The LAYERS table moved to the shared TBox at v1.66.0 -- code-abundance-
#  rdodi found, by reproducing the failure directly, that this data living
#  only here left backlog_lineage_completeness_v1_1_0.py unusable by any
#  adopter, since the tool's own glob only ever searches
#  01-ontologies/backlog_tbox_v*.ttl. See the current backlog_tbox_v*.ttl for the
#  18 individuals, MetricObservation's own layerTier corrected from L2 to
#  L4 in the same move (found and independently re-verified against
#  MetricObservationShape's real severity, not taken on the finding alone).






#################################################################
#  v6.3.0 — It8 re-groomed before building, then delivered.
#
#  RE-GROOMING, per the owner's model. It7 taught one thing the
#  original grooming did not know: a table exported to the ontology
#  and still present in python counts as NOT exported, because the
#  script has not changed. That makes story 2 the whole of the
#  export, not a tidy-up after story 1.
#
#  The re-grooming is recorded as a second RefinementEvent rather
#  than an edit to the first: the earlier analysis was made in good
#  faith on what was known then, and overwriting it would erase the
#  fact that the plan changed for a reason.
#################################################################





#  Label and tier now travel with the rest of each layer's definition in
#  the shared TBox, not as a separate patch here (see the note above).






#################################################################
#  v6.4.0 — the table migration re-planned as batches of four.
#
#  TEST-DRIVEN, and the baseline is kept rather than overwritten.
#
#  BASELINE (recorded, not deleted): one table per story, 3 points
#  each. 22 tables = 66 points = 7.3 iterations = 15 weeks, ending
#  late April against a 31 January deadline. Not reachable, which
#  is what the 30 November checkpoint reported.
#
#  RISK, measured rather than asserted. A story is Done or not; the
#  objective counts tables. A blocked story hides:
#      batch  1  ->  5%% of the work
#      batch  4  -> 18%%
#      batch  8  -> 36%%
#      batch 11  -> 50%%
#  Concealment scales linearly with batch size. Four is the knee:
#  it fits the deadline in two iterations and hides 18 percent
#  rather than 36 or 50.
#
#  Two further risks the probe surfaced, both mitigated rather than
#  accepted: the It8 fallback trap recurs once per reader changed,
#  and one RefinementEvent cannot record eight per-table findings
#  when the LAYERS export produced one. Each batch story therefore
#  carries a criterion PER TABLE and a refinement of its own.
#################################################################










#################################################################
#  A NEW EPIC, not a reopened one.
#
#  EP_CodeTables closed at It8 having delivered exactly what it
#  said: the first table exported and its reader changed. Adding
#  six stories to it would reopen a Done epic and make its
#  completion retroactively untrue — the register rejected it, and
#  the rejection is right. What was learned is that one epic could
#  not hold 23 tables; that is a new epic, at a new estimate.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v6.5.0 — It9 was overfilled and nothing said so.
#
#  The re-plan at v1.113.0 put two batch stories into It9 on top of
#  the three already there: 15 points against a capacity of 9. The
#  capacity clause passed, because it compares hasCommittedEffort
#  against hasCapacity and BOTH ARE ASSERTED. Adding stories does
#  not change the declared commitment.
#
#  Same defect as an ungrounded practice, one level along: a number
#  stated rather than derived, agreeing with another stated number,
#  and nothing comparing either to the work. It10 was caught last
#  release only because its DECLARED figure was raised; It9 was not,
#  because it was not.
#
#  CommittedEffortShape now derives the sum from the contents.
#  Cascaded rather than compressed: the batch stories move one box
#  later each, and It12 opens. The migration finishes 24 January
#  instead of 10 January — still inside the 31 January deadline,
#  and the honest date rather than the one that fitted.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v6.6.0 — the calendar was fiction. Re-based on measurement.
#
#  OWNER FINDING, and it is exact. Measured from the publish
#  commits: It7 took 32 minutes and It8 took 28. The register
#  declared FOURTEEN DAYS each and scheduled the remaining work for
#  November through January, while the day was the 27th of August.
#  Overstatement: 667 times.
#
#  Nothing caught it. iterationStart and iterationEnd are asserted
#  dateTimes and no clause compared them to anything that happened
#  — the same defect as committed effort, one release earlier: a
#  number stated rather than derived.
#
#  Re-based: the two closed iterations carry their MEASURED
#  durations and their real commit-bounded spans. The four open
#  iterations run thirty minutes each from now, which is the mean
#  of what the two closed ones actually took. Objective deadline
#  moves from 31 January to 08:00 today, and the checkpoints with
#  it — the whole migration is about two hours of work, not five
#  months.
#################################################################


#  It1 and It2 predate any measurement. Their durations are
#  recorded as UNMEASURABLE rather than invented: they closed
#  before this framework tracked publish commits per iteration, and
#  writing a plausible figure now would be exactly the fiction this
#  release removes.
fw:QM_ClassRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "ClassRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive true .
fw:QM_AveragePopulation a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "AveragePopulation" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive true .
fw:QM_RelationshipRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "RelationshipRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive false .
fw:QM_AttributeRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "AttributeRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive false .
fw:QM_InheritanceRichness a backlog:QualityMetric ;
    backlog:hasMetricName "InheritanceRichness" ; backlog:isPopulationSensitive false .

#################################################################
#  It9 delivered. Three stories, three findings.
#################################################################





#################################################################
#  Every timestamp re-based to real time.
#
#  Found while measuring It9: the objective baselines were dated
#  26 OCTOBER — two months in the FUTURE — so a real observation
#  taken today sorted BEFORE its own baseline and every objective
#  read at its starting value. Three met became three met by
#  accident; four unmet were unmeasurable.
#
#  The v1.115.0 re-basing corrected the iteration calendar and left
#  the observation and refinement timestamps in the invented one. A
#  partial re-basing is worse than none: it puts real and fictional
#  dates in the same ordering and nothing in a timestamp says which
#  is which.
#################################################################

#  Baselines dated to the Objective stage, not to the moment of
#  re-basing. The blanket substitution put every baseline at 05:50
#  today — AFTER the It7 and It8 deliveries that moved them — so
#  two objectives read their own starting value with the delivery
#  sorted behind it. A baseline is taken when the objective is set,
#  and the Objective stage closed at commit 0b87c6a.


#################################################################
#  v6.8.0 — the table population, counted and classified.
#
#  It10 began by counting what remained and found 26 tables, not
#  22. The count GREW because this lineage shipped new scripts
#  carrying new tables — an objective whose denominator moves
#  cannot be met, and nothing was watching the denominator.
#
#  Of the 26, only 14 hold classification knowledge. The rest are
#  operational: paths, globs, file extensions, which directories a
#  build drops. Ruled out by three agreeing tests recorded in the
#  TBox — the decisive one being that exporting them would ADD
#  classes the exported material does not require, breaching
#  Obj_NoNewClasses in the act of satisfying Obj_TablesExported.
#
#  Every table is registered below with its kind and whether it has
#  moved, so the objective counts a named population rather than a
#  regex over capitalised names.
#################################################################
fw:Tbl_SCAN_coverage_gate a backlog:CodeTable ;
    backlog:tableInModule "backlog_coverage_gate_v1_1_1.py" ;
    backlog:hasTableKind backlog:Table_Operational ;
    backlog:tableExported false .
fw:Tbl_PUBLIC_ONLY_distribution_drift_c a backlog:CodeTable ;
    backlog:tableInModule "backlog_distribution_drift_check_v1_0_0.py" ;
    backlog:hasTableKind backlog:Table_Operational ;
    backlog:tableExported false .
fw:Tbl_VOLATILE_distribution_drift_c a backlog:CodeTable ;
    backlog:tableInModule "backlog_distribution_drift_check_v1_0_0.py" ;
    backlog:hasTableKind backlog:Table_Operational ;
