#################################################################
#  v8.0.0 — MAJOR. A lineage becomes a thing.
#
#  Seven lineages ran in one register file and every one was
#  validated on every release. They are near-disjoint — 22
#  cross-lineage references, all memberOfContainer into a shared
#  container — yet none could be set aside, because A LINEAGE WAS
#  A PATTERN OF LINKS AND NOT AN OBJECT.
#
#  Partitioning by inferring the closure broke 297 constraints:
#  harnesses, refinements and planning events had no lineage of
#  their own and stayed behind while the items they pointed at
#  moved. That is the modelling gap, and belongsToLineage closes
#  it by being carried by EVERY individual rather than only by
#  work items.
#
#  Computed, not guessed: 1137 individuals assigned by walking out
#  from each mission and following both directions of every
#  structural edge. 114 are FRAMEWORK-WIDE — code tables,
#  governance rulings, lineage layers, quality metrics — because
#  they describe the framework itself and belong to no single run.
#  Ten stage outputs and plan artefacts of the first lineage were
#  assigned by reading them.
#################################################################

#  Lineage membership, one line per individual.


#  Gate K caught two defects in one header. The v7.12.0 bump never
#  landed — the block still read 7.11.0, so the 8.0.0 replacement
#  matched nothing and silently did nothing, while the FILENAME
#  moved to 8.0.0 and the changelog announced it. Two releases were
#  described as versions the file did not claim.
#
#  And owl:priorVersion carried TWO values on a functional
#  property, from a bump whose replacement also missed. A chain of
#  versions that forks is not a chain.
#
#  Both found by a gate whose whole job is comparing three
#  statements of the same number — the kind of check that looks
#  redundant until the day the three disagree.

#################################################################
#  v8.1.0 — the mission statement, read back, settles the floor.
#
#  The owner asked me to read the mission statements rather than
#  the counters. Mission_OntologyDriven says:
#
#    "What remains as prose EXPLAINS; what remains as code executes
#     standard engines. NEITHER CARRIES MEANING THAT ONLY THEY
#     DEFINE."
#
#  Obj_RowsUnchecked counts rows whose first cell does not resolve
#  to a TBox term. That is a PROXY for the mission clause and not
#  the clause itself, and the proxy and the clause disagree on the
#  last fifteen rows.
#
#  Tested one by one against the actual clause — is this row's
#  meaning defined anywhere but the prose?
#
#    the fit-gap gate passes on measured        AdaptationGate, gatePassed
#    a gate whose result contradicts            the gatePassed clause
#    digests catch fabrication                  StageOutput, hasStateDigest
#    digests miss backwards construction        closedAtCommit
#    order needs an external witness            closedAtCommit
#    the witness has a measured limit           hasDurationSource
#    the facing rows                            GoalFacing
#    the five artefact-file rows                ArtifactEvidence, Manifest
#    every item traces to an objective          pursuesObjective + L4 clause
#    every epic decomposes                      decomposesInto, EpicSpecifiedShape
#    no item pursues an out-of-scope objective  fillsScope + clause
#
#  ELEVEN of eleven. Every remaining row EXPLAINS something the
#  ontology already defines and enforces. Not one carries meaning
#  that only it defines.
#
#  So the objective is MET on its mission's terms and its counter
#  reads 15 on a proxy that was never the requirement. Recorded as
#  Ach_Retrospective — the count stays at 15 permanently rather
#  than being adjusted, because adjusting it would be the exact
#  fabrication this framework exists to catch.
#################################################################



#  Historical: fw:Mission_OntologyDriven was corrected here (v1.170.0-era)
#  from a premature Out_Achieved (set 2026-08-27, before this mission's own
#  CheckpointObservedShape existed to catch it) to Out_InFlight, with the
#  rationale: "Corrected from a premature Out_Achieved... Six of seven
#  objectives held or improved to target. The seventh, Obj_RulingsQueryable,
#  was genuinely at target (0) as of the It7 observation, but
#  Metric_Obj_RulingsQueryable's own counting method continued to count
#  every governance ruling authored afterward... Re-observed 2026-09-03:
#  38, a real regression from 0, honestly recorded rather than adjusted...
#  the mission stays InFlight rather than forcing either." Superseded by
#  the real, later closure recorded where fw:Mission_OntologyDriven is
#  declared above -- kept here as history, not as an active triple, per
#  this project's own no-multi-version-clutter convention: a changed
#  value gets a new record, the old one is not deleted, but only one may
#  be the active, asserted outcome at a time. Lineage archival is a
#  separate, later decision from mission closure and is not set here.


#################################################################
#  v8.2.0 — every container states its scope.
#
#  Assigned by reading MEMBERSHIP, not by naming: a container whose
#  members all belong to one lineage is lineage-scoped; one whose
#  members span lineages, or which has none, is framework-scoped.
#  Computed rather than listed, because the last hand-written
#  classification in this package was wrong six times out of six.
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v9.0.0 — six lineages set down.
#
#  807 individuals and 7071 triples moved to
#  backlog_framework_archive_abox_v1_0_0.ttl, which the ordinary
#  validation path does not load. That is the point: finished work
#  was being re-checked by 299 SPARQL constraints on every release.
#
#  The Lineage individuals STAY LIVE. They are the index, and an
#  index that archives with its contents cannot be searched. Each
#  now names the file its contents went to, so a reader can find
#  what was set aside rather than discover that it vanished.
#################################################################



#  Scope, Roadmap, Commitment_Dev and DoD moved to the archive
#  after the split, on evidence rather than on the earlier rule.
#
#  I had scoped them FRAMEWORK-WIDE because they span several
#  lineages. Measured after the move: Scope holds 14 references and
#  ZERO to live work, Roadmap 12 and zero, the other two none at
#  all. Spanning several lineages does not make a container
#  framework-wide when every one of those lineages is retired — it
#  makes it a retired container with a wide reach.
#
#  The rule is corrected: a container is framework-scoped when it
#  serves the LIVE lineage, not when it once served many.

#  The live register needs its own commitment. Commitment_Dev went
#  to the archive with the lineage it was made for, and nothing
#  replaced it — the clause caught that a backlog with no committed
#  goal is a list. Written now rather than left, because the live
#  lineage HAS a goal and simply never recorded the commitment to
#  it separately from the objectives underneath.

#################################################################
#  v9.1.0 — the archive flags said both true and false.
#
#  Found by reading the PUBLISHED files rather than trusting last
#  release's report. Every Lineage carried lineageArchived false
#  from its declaration AND true from the block appended when the
#  archive was written. Both on a FUNCTIONAL property, and no
#  clause noticed: owl:FunctionalProperty is a statement OWL
#  reasoners enforce and SHACL does not, and this suite is SHACL.
#
#  Consequence: the six archived lineages reported themselves as
#  not archived to any query that took the first value. The archive
#  file was real, the split was real, and the register said it had
#  not happened.
#
#  Third time this session a functional property has carried two
#  values — hasInvariantStatus twice, and priorVersion once. The
#  pattern is always an append that should have been a replace.
#################################################################


#################################################################
#  v9.2.0 — Inv_ClauseProven worked down, and a naming defect
#  caught along the way that made the first measurement lie.
#
#  A fixture proving 25 targeted clauses was written, and the
#  clause-proof checker filters fixtures by FILENAME — it only
#  scans files containing "negative", "adversarial" or
#  "digestfail". The fixture was named fixture_sparse_shapes and
#  the checker never read it: proven fixtures were shown firing by
#  direct invocation while the tool's own count stayed at 112,
#  because the tool was silently ignoring the very file meant to
#  move it.
#
#  Caught by comparing a TRUE baseline (fixture entirely removed)
#  against the measured after-state, rather than trusting a single
#  number — the two were identical, which is the sign a comparison
#  is broken rather than a sign nothing moved.
#
#  Renamed to fixture_sparse_shapes_negative and re-measured:
#################################################################



#################################################################
#  v9.3.0 — ToolScript declarations, each individually verified.
#
#  Ten checkers verified TRUE by running them bare and reading the
#  usage error or its equivalent. Fourteen verified FALSE the same
#  way -- confirmed reading real self-located data, not guessed
#  from source shape or from a bare-run PASS test, both of which
#  were tried in this release and both of which were wrong.
#
#  backlog_clause_proof and backlog_release_metrics did not
#  complete within the probing window and are declared with
#  hasRationale noting the gap rather than a guessed boolean.
#################################################################

