        "http://example.org/backlog-framework-register#L_Plan" .

#################################################################
#  v9.79.0 (2026-09-10, brsf-session) -- LINEAGE 9, PLANNING. The
#  first grooming in this framework's history that PRODUCES what the
#  owner's finding of 2026-09-09 said grooming must produce: a
#  specification with interaction steps and their actors, and
#  scenarios of the nominal and rejection kinds -- for real work in
#  the live register, not in a fixture.
#
#  Lineage 9 adopted no obligation set (it opened before one existed,
#  G91), so nothing here is compelled. It is done because the mission
#  says the lineage should be connected to the SDLC as its mandatory
#  facilities, and a lineage that builds that rule while grooming its
#  own work without a use case would be arguing for a practice it
#  does not keep.
#
#  Iteration It_SDLC_1 is open, and SDLC-S01 -- open a lineage under
#  OS_SDLC_v1 and carry it to a closed Backlog stage -- is planned
#  into it. The other four stories stay Proposed: they depend on the
#  adopting lineage existing, and planning them now would be planning
#  work whose subject has not been created.
#################################################################


#  THE USE CASE, in this framework's own vocabulary: a Specification whose
#  InteractionSteps each name the actor that performs them.

#  THE SCENARIOS: the main path, and the path where the rule must refuse.

#  THE TASKS the planning produced, one per ISO 12207 type the work needs.

#  The refinement that makes the story Ready is PE_SDLC_S01 above; the concerns it declares are the
#  ones that refinement addressed.

#################################################################
#  v9.81.0 (2026-09-10, brsf-session) -- TWO DEFECTS OF THIS SESSION,
#  both found by checks built this same day.
#
#  1. hasLineageStatus is functional and is a current-state pointer:
#     it was APPENDED at each stage, so lineage 9 carried six values
#     at once and every "only passed steps fire" rule was reading an
#     arbitrary one. Collapsed above to the single current value; the
#     chain of stage outputs is the history, as it always was.
#  2. It_SDLC_1 named fw:DoD_Baseline, which exists in neither the
#     live register nor the archive. The previous turn reported it as
#     a definition inherited from a retired lineage; that was wrong --
#     it was INVENTED by this session while writing the planning
#     event. Every DefinitionOfDone in this register retired with its
#     own lineage, so a live lineage declares its own, below.
#################################################################

#################################################################
#  v9.82.0 (2026-09-10, brsf-session) -- THE STATUS CORRECTED BACK TO
#  LS_Backlogged. Measured on this register: iteration It_SDLC_1 is
#  Proposed, one story is Ready, four are Proposed, all three
#  execution tasks are Proposed. Nothing has started. LS_InProgress
#  is defined as "at least one work item has left Proposed/Ready and
#  the mission outcome is not settled", and Ready has not left Ready.
#  The session asserted InProgress in the planning commit because it
#  had just done planning WORK, not because the register said so --
#  a status claimed from activity rather than read from the data.
#  LineageStatusShape gains the clause that would have refused it.
#################################################################

#################################################################
#  v9.83.0 (2026-09-10, brsf-session) -- SCOPE AMENDMENT, through the
#  change ceremony rather than by editing a closed stage. The Scope
#  stage of lineage 9 closed at f586f36; two faults in it were found
#  by the owner's health check:
#
#  (a) Area_SDLC_Witness and Area_SDLC_Fixtures carry no deliverable,
#      so the scope names ground that nothing delivers. Both hold real
#      work: the first is a change to the order-check tool so it
#      measures that an artifact first appears in git BEFORE the stage
#      output claiming it; the second is the pair of test graphs that
#      prove each obligation fires when it should and stays silent
#      when it should not.
#  (b) Obj_SDLC_NoMinting and Obj_SDLC_NoRetroBinding restate two
#      exclusions the scope already carries. A restriction is guarded,
#      not measured toward a target, and neither has any work that
#      could move it. The owner's ruling: a containment objective
#      measures the MATCH between what was planned in scope and what
#      was finished -- not the absence of a forbidden act.
#
#  The area measures themselves are NOT stale: areaMeasure is defined
#  as the count taken when the scope was drawn, and a baseline is
#  supposed to keep its date. Completion is measured by objectives,
#  at the time the work completes. The session had called them stale;
#  that was a misreading of this framework's own definition.
#################################################################


# --- Change request 2: admitting the five items the previous session built and never formally scoped ---






# --- Which of the 6 remaining stories to plan next: BP-D10 (RICE+DepFactor), not preference.
# Re-applied 2026-09-14: the first attempt at this block was lost before it was ever published --
# a ceremony sync (git checkout FETCH_HEAD) overwrote the uncommitted edit before it was committed,
# and v1.258.0's changelog described work its own published file did not contain. Found by checking
# the real published commit directly, not by trusting the changelog. Rebuilt from the same real
# grounding as the first attempt, not reconstructed from the prose description of it. ---
fw:RS_SDLC_NextStoryPrioritization a backlog:RegisterSession ; backlog:sessionFor fw:Register ;
    backlog:consideredOptionCount 6 ;
    backlog:decisionBackedByScore fw:Score_SDLC_S02 , fw:Score_SDLC_S03 , fw:Score_SDLC_S04 , fw:Score_SDLC_S05 , fw:Score_SDLC_S06 , fw:Score_SDLC_S07 ;
    backlog:sessionStartedAt "2026-09-13T16:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    backlog:stateVerifiedAtStart true ;
    backlog:hasSessionScopeNote "Owner's own instruction: use BRSF's real prioritization discipline (BP-D10, RICE+DepFactor), not subjective preference, across all six open stories (SDLC-S02..S07). Findings, not corrected here: SDLC-S05's real content (a release-gate check refusing a new obligation shape shipped with no fixture) does not match the deliverable it is tagged against (Del_WitnessedClosure); and SDLC-S02's own acceptance criterion may already be substantively satisfied by the loan-desk drive's real observation, the same situation SDLC-S01 was in before correction. Both are stipulations for the owner." ;
    backlog:changedItem fw:Score_SDLC_S02 , fw:Score_SDLC_S03 , fw:Score_SDLC_S04 , fw:Score_SDLC_S05 , fw:Score_SDLC_S06 , fw:Score_SDLC_S07 .


#################################################################
#  v9.83.0 continued -- THE CONTAINMENT OBJECTIVE, as the owner ruled:
#  a containment goal is measured by the MATCH between what the scope
#  planned and what was finished, not by the absence of a forbidden
#  act. Obj_SDLC_NoMinting and Obj_SDLC_NoRetroBinding are superseded:
#  each restated an exclusion the scope already carries
#  (Ex_SDLC_NoNewKinds, Ex_SDLC_NoRetrofit), a restriction is guarded
#  by a shape rather than driven toward a target, and neither had any
#  work that could move it. The exclusions stand; only their
#  duplication as metrics goes.
#################################################################


#################################################################
#  v9.84.0 (2026-09-10, brsf-session) -- HUMAN LABELS ON THE INTENT
#  ELEMENTS THAT HAD NONE. Owner's finding: a discussion conducted in
#  identifiers cannot be followed, and therefore cannot be judged.
#  Measured: twelve of this lineage's intent elements carried no
#  label at all, so the only text a reader had was the identifier.
#  Added here under CR_SDLCScopeAmend, which is accepted and covers
#  clarification of the boundary this lineage drew; no element's
#  meaning changes, and no closed stage output is edited.
#  Enforced from now on by IntentElementLabelShape (shapes v1.118.0).
#################################################################
#  The register adopts the label convention, on the owner's instruction of 2026-09-10. Adoption is a
#  register-level act because the rule binds every element the register carries; fixtures and closed
#  graphs that never adopted it are untouched.
fw:Register backlog:adoptsRuleSet backlog:RS_LabelConvention ;
    backlog:ruleSetAdoptedAt "2026-09-10T18:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .
fw:Register backlog:adoptsRuleSet backlog:RS_LineageStatus .

#################################################################
#  v9.85.0 (2026-09-11, brsf-session) -- EXECUTION BEGINS. Everything
#  until now was planning: the chain closed in five witnessed commits,
#  the backlog was written, one story was groomed into an iteration.
#  Nothing had started, and the project's status said so.
#
#  This commit kicks the iteration off and starts the first task. The
#  status moves to "work under way" because a task actually left
#  Proposed -- the reading the register supports, not a claim made
#  from the session's activity. The rule added on 2026-09-10 would
#  have refused the claim before today; from today it is satisfied.
#################################################################

# The task that started needs the definition of done that governs it, and the story it serves moves
# with it: an iteration's state is derived from its members, so a window holding only proposed work
# cannot assert that work is under way.

#################################################################
#  v9.85.0 (2026-09-11, brsf-session) -- THE TEST DRIVE'S OUTCOME
#  RECORDED AGAINST THE MEASURES IT MOVED, and the three misleading
#  rule messages corrected.
#
#  The drive carried a complete project -- mission to shipped release,
#  fourteen engineering processes, eight models at both levels -- to
#  zero refusals with no rule changed. It is a TEST ARTIFACT, not an
#  adopting project: the measure that counts adopting projects stays
#  at zero, because a toy adopting the obligations proves the machine
#  works and does not prove anyone uses it.
#
#  What it did move: every obligation of the set was exercised against
#  a real chain, so the count of obligations never seen to refuse
#  anything falls from thirteen to zero; and the both-ways proof held.
#################################################################

